The project aims to develop country's first Biodiversity Finance Plan, by analyzing existing expenditures, assessing financial needs, and developing innovative solutions such as green taxes, dedicated funds, and payments for ecosystem services. The development of the Biodiversity Finance Plan stems from both national and international obligations, including commitments under the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) and the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework.

The BFP will:
• Quantify current biodiversity-related expenditures and estimate the finance required to achieve national and global biodiversity targets;
• Map financing mechanisms, including subsidies with potential harmful impacts;
• Propose a set of priority finance solutions with the strongest potential, feasibility, and biodiversity impact;
• Be endorsed by the Government and integrated into national strategies such as the NBSAP, as well as broader agendas on sustainable development, climate change, land and water management.

The project will specifically focus on:
